AB  - Domain adaptation techniques aim to transfer knowledge from a labeled source domain to an unlabeled target domain, thereby reducing the reliance on target domain labels. However, in cross-machine scenarios, substantial task-irrelevant interference often degrades diagnostic accuracy. To address this, a dual-conditional feature constraints based tri-component decoupling neural network (DFC-TDNN) is proposed. First, a tri-component decoupling neural network (TDNN) is constructed to disentangle domain-invariant features through a three-branch separation strategy across convolutional channels. Meanwhile, a cross-domain distribution-aware minimum class confusion (CDMCC) loss is proposed to guide the model in learning more discriminative target features by leveraging the spatial relationship between class centers in the source and target domains. Finally, the dual-classifier structure is integrated with CDMCC to form a dual-conditional feature constraints module, which further promotes the extraction of domain-invariant features adaptable to different decision boundaries. Experiments on 12 cross-machine fault diagnostic tasks across four datasets demonstrate that the proposed method effectively extracts domain-invariant features and achieves significantly higher diagnostic accuracy compared to existing approaches.
