AB  - In order to optimize the performance of controllers, the optimal trade-off between settling-time (ST) and energy consumption has become a worthwhile and meaningful issue in control engineering. To address this issue, a fixed-time (FXT) event-triggered controller for stochastic coupled neural networks (CNNs) is designed to achieve the optimal tradeoff between ST and energy consumption. First, a FXT event-triggered control strategy is proposed to ensure that the system achieves FXT synchronization, which enables the system to have less ST and lower energy consumption during the synchronization process. Second, the Zeno effect can be effectively eliminated, and there is a strict positive lower bound on the triggering time, which not only ensures the feasibility of the event-triggered mechanism but also avoids the resource consumption problem caused by infinitely frequent triggering. Third, a linear normalization method has been proposed to ensure optimal ST and energy consumption of the system, which can ensure an efficient tradeoff between system performance and resource utilization. The effectiveness of the proposed approach is validated through numerical simulations and comparative evaluations.
