AB  - Traditional asphalt mixture design relies on empirical testing, which is time-consuming and provides limited quantitative control over structural configuration. This study applies complex network theory to develop a model quantifying the multi-level structure of asphalt mixtures. The model represents aggregates as nodes and interactions as weighted edges, capturing contact, local, and system-level interactions. A composition-structure model is trained using machine learning, combined with explainable artificial intelligence (AI) techniques shapley additive explanations and partial dependence plot, which together improve the accuracy and efficiency of asphalt mixture structure design. Benchmarking six machine learning algorithms identified extreme gradient boosting as the top performer; after hyperparameter tuning, it served as the backbone for explainable analyses. Structural networks show that large particles form a strong backbone with short transfer paths, while smaller particles cluster around it, relaying loads locally. This creates a hierarchical system linking global transfer to fine-grained particle distribution. The 2.36–4.75 mm fraction improves global connectivity and local stability but should be limited to 8–20 % to avoid reducing transfer capacity. Aggregates sized 4.75–9.5 mm reduce local connectivity and transfer efficiency; limiting this fraction to ≤38 % preserves stable load distribution. Aggregates sized 9.5–16 mm are essential for forming the primary skeleton and stable local configurations. Higher asphalt content reduces contact strength and hampers stable configuration. These findings highlight the potential of AI-enhanced design approach for engineering applications, advancing the transition of asphalt mixture design from an empirical approach to a more automated, scientific methodology.
