KW  - Deep learning
AB  - Accurate prediction of deep-sea sediment concentrations, including iron (Fe), manganese (Mn), calcium (Ca), and titanium (Ti), plays an important role in understanding geochemical processes and constitutes resources with substantial economic potential. In this study a novel clustered machine learning (CML) technique is develop to predict the concentration of elements in deep-sea sediments. Four CMLs, including clustered multi-linear regression (CMLR), clustered shallow neural network (CSNN), clustered deep neural network (CDNN), and clustered adaptive neuro-fuzzy inference system (CANFIS), were developed and applied to model the concentrations of Fe, Mn, Ca, and Ti under two different scenarios. In the first scenario, the training data contain some information from the sample profiles, while in the second scenario, the elements of a sample profile, which were completely excluded from the training process, were simulated. Results show that the developed CML method significantly enhances the accuracy of the prediction of the concentration of deep-sea minerals compared to standard models, improving the root mean square error (RMSE) by approximately 121 %. While it was relatively easy to predict Ca, the prediction of Mn is far more difficult and only the developed CML models provide acceptable accuracy for Mn (R2 > 0.74). The developed CML models also predict Fe, Ca, and Ti, far more accurate than standard models even in out-of-training profiles. These findings demonstrate that the developed CML technique provides a robust and reliable tool for the modeling of mineral distributions in complex deep-sea environments and potential applications in resource exploration and environmental assessments.
