KW  - Generative adversarial networks
KW  - Time series prediction
KW  - Deep learning
AB  - Accurate daily carbon emission forecasting is critical for high-energy-consuming enterprises to achieve low-carbon operations and support data-driven production and carbon asset management. Reliable predictions also provide essential guidance for optimizing emission reduction strategies and advancing climate actions. However, due to indirect accounting nature of carbon emission and the complexity of multiple real-world influencing factors, carbon emission data often exhibit nonlinear characteristics, long-tail distributions, and abnormal fluctuations, which pose challengs to the robustness of existing prediction methods. To address the issues of distribution mismatch and error accumulation in deep learning-based time series prediction, this study proposes a Carbon Emission Generative Adversarial Network (CEGAN) framework, which captures complex data distributions and generates sequences consistent with real data statistical properties through adversarial learning. The generator integrates a multi-objective loss function, comprising adversarial loss, mean squared error (MSE) loss, and an adaptive smoothed peak penalty, to balance generation fidelity, predictive accuracy, and emission reduction constraints. Furthermore, the model modulates stochastic noise with energy and economic knowledge, enhancing its capability to model nonlinear dependencies between external drivers and carbon emissions. Experimental evaluations on multiple provincial and national carbon emission datasets demonstrate that CEGAN achieves robust and accurate forecasting performance, outperforming existing benchmark models across multiple metrics.
