AB  - Accurate traffic forecasting is essential for intelligent transportation systems. However, most existing studies model only a single modality, either traffic speed or traffic volume, overlooking their joint dynamics. This work investigates Graph WaveNet (GWNet) for traffic volume prediction and introduces a bidirectional integration framework that jointly models speed and volume within graph convolution and gated temporal modules. Experiments on three real-world datasets (PEMSD4, PEMSD8, and DUBLIN2021) show that GWNet achieves state-of-the-art performance, reducing mean absolute error (MAE) and root mean squared error (RMSE) by up to 12.3% and 15.7%, respectively, compared to ten baselines. Our bidirectional analysis reveals an asymmetric relationship: volume consistently improves speed prediction, while speed benefits volume forecasting only under certain network conditions. We further propose a node pruning strategy that reduces training time by 21.8% with minimal accuracy loss. These results provide insights for effective multimodal fusion and spatiotemporal modeling in traffic forecasting.
