AB  - State-of-the-art capabilities of search engines have evolved with the introduction of Artificial Intelligence (AI), which has made information retrieval even more complicated and context-aware. However, higher energy consumption, which results from the increasing processing requests of AI-powered search engines, is causing higher carbon emissions and worsening environmental conditions. This article evaluates the environmental impact of AI-driven search technologies and proposes an integrated technique that combines hardware optimization, energy-aware algorithms, and the integration of renewable energy. The assessment and effectiveness of the proposed framework were evaluated by a mixed-methods approach that leverages the Microsoft AI for Earth dataset to benchmark energy consumption patterns and the Google Carbon-Aware Computing Application Program Interface to simulate renewable energy availability across regions, comparative analysis, energy consumption data, and computational simulations. It was found that the AI-powered search engines based on the proposed techniques are showing significant improvements: while maintaining 98% of the search accuracy, there was a 40% energy reduction, and adding 20% energy savings by Graphics Processing Unit (GPU). The key role in this will be played by integrating renewable resources, with a potential of a 50% reduction in carbon emissions, making task-offloading 30% faster, saving 30% of energy in the optimal range, and a 40% decrease in the environmental impact score.
