AB  - This study addresses the challenges posed by the integration of photovoltaic (PV) units into distribution systems, leading to operational disruptions such as increased losses, voltage violations, and overloaded lines. To maximize PV hosting capacity while addressing these issues, a probabilistic method is introduced, considering the presence of electric vehicles (EVs). The researchers propose a double-layered approach involving coordinated management of on-load tap changers (OLTCs), PV inverters, and EVs, aiming to enhance the charge/discharge dynamics of EVs, OLTC taps, and reactive power. The optimization employs an enhanced particle swarm method with weighted delay velocity for efficient global and local searches. Three strategies are evaluated, revealing significant improvements in voltage profiles during the night in the second and third strategies. OLTC contributions in the second strategy and combined OLTC and VAR resource effects in the third strategy contribute to these enhancements. Additionally, injected/absorbed reactive power by inverters proves influential in voltage regulation and increasing PV hosting capacity. The third strategy, incorporating OLTC and inverters, notably improves hosting capacity by 50 % compared to the first strategy, optimizing system performance by approximately 10 % in terms of voltage profiles.
