AB  - Transfer learning has achieved remarkable success in the field of variable working condition machinery equipment fault diagnosis. However, in practical engineering, the performance of fault diagnosis methods has sharply declined due to their application on bearings of different machines. Moreover, most transfer fault diagnosis methods have poor interpretability to the extent that their performance is not convincing in practical applications. To improve the domain adaptation capability and interpretability, a novel physics-informed blind wavelet deconvolution transfer network (PIBWDTN) is developed for cross-machine bearing fault diagnosis. Firstly, an adaptive blind wavelet deconvolution physics-informed neural network is proposed to extract time-frequency physics features that is capable of fully indicating the health status of the bearings. Secondly, a novel physics-informed modeling loss based on blind wavelet deconvolution is designed to provide additional information about the bearing fault being modeled. Finally, with the help of adaptive blind wavelet deconvolution and physics-informed modeling loss, the domain adaptation stage improves the transfer ability and strengthens the interpretability. The performance of PIBWDTN has been validated on cross-machine fault diagnosis experiments composed of three bearing datasets. The results indicate that PIBWDTN is capable of focusing on the physical information related to bearing faults. The PIBWDTN can enhance the adaptability of cross-machine fault diagnosis, and provide the interpretability for transferable features.
