KW  - Pollutant variables
KW  - Meteorological factors
KW  - Artificial intelligence
KW  - Extreme gradient boosting
KW  - K-nearest neighbour
KW  - Random forest
AB  - Air pollution refers to any chemical, physical, or biological contamination that contaminates an interior or outdoor environment and modifies the intrinsic qualities of the atmosphere. It can be produced by natural or anthropogenic activities. Among those pollutants mentioned by the World Health Organisation (WHO), ground-level ozone (O3), also known as tropospheric ozone, possesses a significant impact on human life. The current study was developed in response to the need to study ground-level ozone concentrations in the Kolkata district, India. To perform factor analysis in the Kolkata district, ground-based O3 data were collected from the Central Pollution Control Board (CPCB). This study employs artificial intelligence (AI) techniques—Random Forest (RF), k-Nearest Neighbours (KNN), and Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost)—to predict ground-level ozone concentrations in Kolkata using meteorological and air quality data from 2020 to 2023. It was performed to check for correlations of O3 with various meteorological and pollutant variables, based on which it was observed that all the pollutant variables showed a positive correlation with O3. Among the meteorological factors, wind direction (r = 0.29), solar radiation (r = 0.21), and barometric pressure (r = 0.55) showed a positive correlation with O3, while relative humidity, wind speed, and ambient temperature displayed a negative correlation. The machine learning-based XGBoost model was identified as the best-fit model for predicting ozone (R2 = 0.80) against KNN and RF models. A study on feature importance showed that relative humidity plays a significant role in ozone prediction. The findings of this study can assist in identifying the sources and factors responsible for ozone pollution in the Kolkata district. The AI-based framework provides an effective tool for ozone forecasting, supporting early-warning systems and air quality management strategies. This, in turn, can help in devising effective strategies to mitigate the issue and develop policies for the optimal design and planning of Kolkata city to reduce air pollution.
