KW  - Potential vulnerability code slicing
KW  - Vulnerability path slicing
KW  - Vulnerability detection
AB  - Software vulnerabilities are becoming increasingly prevalent, making their detection a critical aspect of information security. However, existing deep learning-based vulnerability detection approaches often struggle with limited feature representation, high computational overhead, and low detection efficiency. To address these challenges, we propose a novel vulnerability detection method, TextCN-RGB, which fuses key code slice Text features with complex network (CN) metrics (K-shell decomposition, Hyperlink-Induced Topic Search (HITS), and Degree centrality) to generate innovative Red–Green–Blue (RGB) image representations, which are then analyzed using Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs). By converting source code into RGB images through this fusion, TextCN-RGB captures rich semantic associations, syntactic dependencies, and logical structures, thereby enhancing vulnerability understanding. Moreover, by removing irrelevant and redundant information from function code, TextCN-RGB reduces feature noise and improves learning efficiency during model training. Finally, TextCN-RGB was evaluated on datasets containing 56,239 vulnerable and 80,123 secure functions, achieving an accuracy of 98.35%, nearly 12% higher than the best-performing baselines. Furthermore, when applied to 102,532 real-world files, it successfully detected 18 actual vulnerabilities, demonstrating its practical effectiveness. In terms of runtime performance, TextCN-RGB outperformed two state-of-the-art image-based systems, running 1.9 times and 2 times faster, respectively. This demonstrates its high efficiency in large-scale vulnerability detection tasks.
