AB  - In this study, an optimization process is employed to assess the efficiency enhancement of a concentrated solar system, incorporating a swirl device. The configuration of this system comprises twelve mirrors surrounding a tube with a trapezoidal cavity, forming a novel unit. Alumina-water is used as the operating fluid, and temperature-dependent formulas are employed for property predictions. SolTrace is utilized to assess absorbed flux distribution and visualize ray paths, while artificial intelligence techniques, specifically a neural network-based model, are applied to simulate and predict system behavior. Finite volume simulations are conducted to investigate the value of entropy generation, along with measuring heat effectiveness. The modeling procedure is validated through a comparison with an experimental publication. The optimization process is implemented employing the Strength Pareto Evolutionary Algorithm, focusing on the geometric parameters of the swirl device. The optimization results illustrate the influence of geometrical values on the unit's effectiveness. The goal of the article is to minimize entropy generation, and friction factor and maximize Nusselt number (Nu) and efficiency (ηth). The results of the mean square error are low, with 0.8737 for Nu, 0.0071 for entropy generation from heat transfer, 0.00011 for entropy generation from friction, 7.0369 ×10−8 for ηth, and 2.5687 ×10−8 for friction factor (f). The R2 value for all of them is 0.99.
