AB  - Three-way decision (TWD) provides an effective framework for addressing uncertain multi-attribute decision-making problems. However, existing TWD models are largely confined to sample-level analysis, leading to two main limitations: (1) computational inefficiency when dealing with large-scale data; (2) sensitivity to noise and outliers, resulting in insufficient decision robustness. To address these challenges, this paper proposes a novel TWD model that integrates Gaussian Spherical Fuzzy Information Granules (GSFIGs). Our model begins with the formal definition and adaptive generation of GSFIGs. In contrast to traditional granular structures, GSFIGs not only provide stronger high-dimensional information representation and global information fusion capabilities, but also flexibly adapt to different data distributions. Subsequently, we introduce three novel measures to derive TWD rules at the GSFIG level, including a preference consistency-based score function, a composite evaluation matrix, and a global conditional probability. These components are designed to preserve the original properties of GSFIGs while enhancing the robustness and holisticity of the evaluation. Finally, a complete sample ranking is derived through a hierarchical evaluation framework encompassing class-level, granular-level, and sample-level assessments. Extensive experiments on seven real-world medical datasets demonstrate the competitiveness and effectiveness of the proposed model. The results show that our model significantly outperforms several state-of-the-art methods in terms of computational efficiency and robustness.
