AB  - In practical industrial processes, it is extremely difficult to collect data of all fault modes and working conditions. This limitation causes data-driven models to lack specific training data, leading to insufficient learning of fault features and unsatisfactory diagnostic performance. In response to these challenges, a cross-scale hybrid contrast network (CSHCN) for generalized zero-shot fault diagnosis of rolling bearing is proposed. In CSHCN, a hybrid space is constructed using fault description space and feature space to characterize the relationship between fault modes. In this hybrid space, fault descriptions guide the generation of features for inaccessible classes to address the problem of missing data. To generate higher-quality fault features, contrastive embedding is performed at two scales: instance and class. Instance-scale embedding enhances feature discriminability by pulling together features from the same class, while class-scale embedding aligns generated features with their corresponding fault descriptors. Training stability is ensured through a Wasserstein distance loss with gradient penalty, which mitigates mode collapse and promotes smooth convergence. The proposed method is demonstrated on multiple data sets and a variety of typical scenarios of zero-shot fault diagnosis. Experimental results show that, compared with other methods, the proposed method can diagnose both accessible and inaccessible fault classes and achieves superior accuracy.
