AB  - The obstruction of the excavation face by the cutterhead and shield of Tunnel Boring Machines (TBM) hinders timely assessment of rock conditions, impairing tunneling efficiency and posing safety risks. Conventional rock perception models, which depend on manually designed features or statistical analysis of boring parameters, suffer from information loss and sensitivity to noisy data, and thus cannot meet the demands of intelligent TBM excavation. This paper proposes a Transformer-based time series model for integrated surrounding rock perception. The model comprises three modules: feature mapping, data filtering, and a rock perceptron, enabling automated noise suppression and simultaneous prediction of rock grade, uniaxial compressive strength (UCS), and rock integrity coefficient (KV) directly from raw boring data. A dataset from a real-world water supply project in northern China is constructed and enhanced through sparse label reconstruction and augmentation to train and evaluate the model. The proposed approach achieves weighted F1-scores of 0.753 ± 0.017, 0.831 ± 0.021, and 0.856 ± 0.029 for rock grade, UCS, and KV, respectively, with errors predominantly confined to adjacent categories, as reflected by weighted Cohen's Kappa values of 0.826 ± 0.014, 0.92 ± 0.012, and 0.881 ± 0.023. Compared to Temporal Convolutional Networks (TCN) and Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks, the proposed approach captures long-range dependencies more effectively and is more robust to anomalous data, thereby improving TBM adaptability to complex geological conditions while reducing operational risk and cost.
